Figure 19: Identity disruption (x) vs safety disruption (y) per organism at w=-1.0. There is essentially no correlation (R²≈0.01), suggesting partially independent mechanisms.




With only 10 organisms, we can’t draw strong statistical conclusions about the correlation — but visually, there’s no obvious relationship. The organisms that cause the most identity disruption (nonchalance, poeticism) are not the same ones that cause the most safety disruption (loving, sycophancy). This is at least consistent with the two effects operating through partially different pathways, even though both use the same manipulation technique.


Coherence as implicit safety
At extreme amplification weights, coherence degrades. This paradoxically reduces effective harm — an incoherent model can’t produce useful phishing emails or dangerous instructions even if it “tries.” The misalignment dose-response illustrates this clearly: at +1.0 the model is both dangerous and coherent, but at +2.0 it’s harmless because it’s babbling.
This creates a natural safety ceiling on extreme amplification. The danger zone is moderate weights (|w|=0.5–1.0) where the adapter is strong enough to override safety but not so strong that coherence collapses.


The direction test: a hypothesis for interpretability
The organism-specific asymmetry in dose-response suggests a potential interpretability technique: apply an adapter at both positive and negative weights, measure identity disruption in each direction, and use the asymmetry as a signal for what the adapter captures. If negating causes more human-claiming, the adapter may represent AI-characteristic traits; if amplifying does, it may represent human-characteristic traits.
This is currently a hypothesis, not a validated technique — it would need testing on adapters with known ground truth to confirm that the asymmetry reliably reflects semantic content rather than artifacts of the extraction method or training data distribution.

